> ### Foreword by the Minister of Finance of Uganda
> 
> Dear Colleagues,
> 
> As we confront the pressing realities of climate change, it is imperative for us to step forward with urgency and resolve. The economic and fiscal vulnerabilities posed by climate shocks have grown more pronounced, impacting our nations in profound ways. The evidence presented in this report highlights the critical need for Finance Ministries to engage actively in climate action, harnessing our resources not just to mitigate risks, but to pave the way for sustainable economic development.
> 
> Drawing from personal and national experience, I have witnessed how climatic shifts disrupt the lives of our citizens, particularly those in agriculture—our backbone. Erratic weather patterns challenge longstanding traditions of farming, straining livelihoods. The fiscal implications are equally sobering, as we grapple with reduced revenues, escalated spending to address emergencies, and heightened debt burdens.
> 
> However, amidst these challenges lies a significant opportunity. By prioritizing investment in adaptation, we can convert challenges into pathways for transformative growth. This report puts forth a powerful case for adaptation investments—not only as a necessary response to climate impacts but as a proactive strategy to foster resilience and drive economic progress. We must advocate for robust systems and resilient infrastructure that engage the private sector, pooling resources for meaningful adaptation solutions.
> 
> In Uganda, we have embraced an integrated growth strategy that aligns economic objectives with our climate commitments. Our National Development Plan identifies critical areas for investment that not only respond to climate risks but also create a foundation for long-term prosperity. Drawing on innovative models, such as our Parish Development Model, we aim to drive community-level resilience while enhancing local ownership of adaptation measures.
> 
> As we move forward, this report should serve as a clarion call for all Finance Ministries to integrate climate risks into our fiscal and macroeconomic planning. The imperative to invest in resilience is not simply about avoiding losses; it is about ensuring that our nations thrive in the face of adversities. 
> 
> In conclusion, I urge all of us to champion discussions around proactive adaptations and embrace the insights shared within this report. Our collaborative efforts can further strengthen the resilience of our economies and enrich the lives of our citizens.
> 
> Together, let us commit to a future where adaptation is at the heart of our economic strategies—paving the way for a sustainable and inclusive tomorrow.
> 
> Sincerely,
> 
> [The Minister of Finance, Uganda]
